+ * HiSilicon HiSTB HDMI transmitter bridge
+ *
+ * The transmitter contains a private DDC controller.  Keep its register
+ * protocol local to this driver and expose sink discovery through the DRM
+ * bridge helpers.
+ *
+ * Forward port of the community histb-linux 6.12.y driver of the same name
+ * (drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/hisilicon/histb-hdmi.c) to 6.18, with ONE design
+ * change for the HiMedia Q10 Pro: it ADOPTS the bootloader's running link.
+ *
+ *  - probe() resets nothing, re-initialises nothing and never switches TMDS off.
+ *    The community probe asserts all three resets and runs the vendor init, which
+ *    blanks the TV the moment the module loads.
+ *  - Until a commit asks for a mode other than the one the bootloader started
+ *    (the display driver names it with histb_hdmi_set_boot_mode()), atomic_pre_enable
+ *    / atomic_enable / atomic_disable / atomic_post_disable do NOTHING to the
+ *    hardware - "link_owned" is false.  Disabling an adopted link never blanks it.
+ *  - The first different mode takes the link over with the community's complete,
+ *    proven sequence (reset bus/core/phy, vendor init, configure) and from then on
+ *    every enable runs it.
+ *  - The pixel clock RATE is set by the CRTC driver before this bridge runs (the
+ *    display timing must follow the PLL, not precede it); this bridge only gates the
+ *    clock and verifies the rate.  If the CRTC could not set it, the bridge gives
+ *    up and puts the previous link back.
+ *  - HDMI audio configured by another driver (q10_aiao) is saved before a TX reset
+ *    and put back after TMDS is up, so a mode switch does not silence it.
+ *
+ * Not tested on hardware.
+ */
